<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7658"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 5:13 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>playing head games</i> <BR> <BR>Actually, friend, it is you who is playing the head games. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7658-->
<!--Post: 7668--><!--Time: 1108651208-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7668"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:newmilleniumwriter@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tippy<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tippy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 6:40 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Bmodule - these &#34;head games&#34; as you like to call them - probably have just the opposite effect on BTK than you are surmising. <BR> <BR>If you think back to his early letter he put forth the question, &#34;How many do I have to kill before I get some news recognition or attention?&#34; &#40;paraphrased&#41;   <BR> <BR>The attention and speculation seems to satiate his thirst for killing, at least in theory. <BR> <BR>Besides, most experts believe that BTK is no longer in the &#39;killing&#39; part of his game.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7668-->
<!--Post: 7669--><!--Time: 1108651540-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7669"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:newmilleniumwriter@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tippy<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tippy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 6:45 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George - perhaps of interest to you, considering your theory of possible geographical aspects of BTK&#39;s profile, I thought you may like to take a look at this link.  I believe it is the same locality where the sheet music you noted was published.  In fact, it mentions that very song in its text. <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kshs.org/cool2/klan.htm" target=_top>http://www.kshs.org/cool2/klan.htm</a> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7669-->
<!--Post: 7782--><!--Time: 1108682999-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7782"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 3:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Kansasgirl, chances are good that you&#39;re right on the money about BTK&#39;s career.  I think it could be a connection between BTK and Joe Otero.  I think they could&#39;ve crossed paths somehow in Wichita, through their jobs.  I don&#39;t think there was a Puerto Rican, or military service, connection there.  I think they met in a more mundane way, and that BTK was playing mind games of some sort to make Joe paranoid, as Charlie remembers it, in the weeks before the crime..  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7782-->
<!--Post: 7785--><!--Time: 1108683249-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7785"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 3:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->George, <BR> <BR>I suspect Beattie&#39;s book was the primary motivation for the reemergence of the BTK Strangler in March 2004. The BTK Strangler believes Beattie&#39;s book violates his intellectual property, such as copyright and trademark. The unknown subject believes, he exclusively owns the narrative and the sobriquet. The BTK Strangler believes Beattie is merely an attorney. He may understand law enforcement and the judiciary, his expertise does not extend to an adequate understanding of psychology, particularly the psychopathic mind and life as a serial murderer. He is outraged the proposed book would be considered the definitive narrative on his life and criminal career. This would be the first book dedicated to the BTK Strangler. The unknown subject would remain anonymous, if no book would be published. If, another writer decided to publish a book on him, he would have responded with a desire to resume dialog with the media and the Wichita Police Department. <BR> <BR>It appears books are an important part of his life. Books symbolize the authority of knowledge. Scholarly argument and theory are contained on the pages of books. Writers and poets have used papyrus to scribe novellas, dramas and sonnets. The unknown subject&#39;s poetry may be questionable, but believes Beattie&#39;s writing is not worthy of publication. <BR> <BR>Your second suggestion seems more likely. The unknown subject does not appreciate inaccuracies or errors in his narrative. It would prompt a response to the media organization. It seems unlikely the BTK Strangler would provide incriminating information. He seems to possess discipline and analyzes his responses before submitting them. <BR> <BR>My perspective on the BTK Strangler&#39;s current strategy can be found below: <BR> <BR>The BTK Strangler is currently engaging in a game of strategic behavior. The unknown subject will resist mentioning unknown victims until Beattie&#39;s book is published. He wants to demonstrate Beattie&#39;s narrative is incomplete, poorly researched and lacks insight. This will be the Nash Equilibrium for the BTK Strangler in this game. This is one of many games the unknown subject would have devised in the short term and the long term. <BR> <BR>The recent flurry of correspondences from the unknown subject may be motivated by a desire to disrupt Beattie&#39;s book. He believes submitting trophies from earlier crime scenes may compel Beattie to revise parts of his book and delay the publication of the book. He is patient and when the book is published will respond by submitting trophies from unknown victims. <BR> <BR>You can submit a message to my electronic mail account. I allow the webmaster to provide my details to you.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7785-->
<!--Post: 7799--><!--Time: 1108686199-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7799"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:newmilleniumwriter@yahoo.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Tippy<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tippy<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 17, 2005 - 4:23 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not a bad guess, Ivy.  I think you are on to something there. <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s return blows Beattie&#39;s book out of the water.  Oh, he may still publish it - but it won&#39;t hold its authoritative value since BTK is proving profilers &#40;and everyone else&#41; wrong at every turn.  And after all the hoopla - the readers KNOW Beattie is speculating.   <BR> <BR>Had BTK not returned - the unauthorized guess may have read as probable truth.  Of course, we don&#39;t know how Beattie is changing the substance - but change it, he must, to some degree.   <BR> <BR>A double-edged sword for Beattie.  Lots of free publicity for his subject and genre but a shadow cast on his substance credibility. <BR> <BR>Time will tell. <BR> <BR>The one think BTK has established - Beattie knows NO more than the PD do.  And that is relatively little. <BR> <BR>BTK&#39;s attention could actually &#39;burn&#39; Beattie&#39;s market if the short attention spans of the public tire of the escapade before print time.   <BR> <BR>Or - if BTK tells all. If those chapter titles contain actual stories and they are published - Beattie&#39;s book is dead in the water.   <BR> <BR>BTK could not profit from his own sales - I&#39;ve heard.  Does anyone know if the profit would go to a victim&#39;s fund?  Seems I read about that somewhere, but it escapes me at present.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 7799-->
<!--Post: 8015--><!--Time: 1108797613-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8015"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 18, 2005 - 11:20 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;BTK&#39;s attention could actually &#39;burn&#39; Beattie&#39;s market if the short attention spans of the public tire of the escapade before print time.&#34;</i> <BR>Tippy, extremely cogent words. Of course it should be big news when a drop occurrs and as far as I&#39;m concerned he&#39;s becme very tiesome. I admit to stentiorian gales of howling when people take BTK at his word or think his comminuiques &#34;mean&#34; anything. One big story; Iraq, a tornado, a terrorist attack, a bad economy, a category five hurricane hitting the US, more war declared, etc., etc. BTK will be dropped like a used banana skin.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8015-->
<!--Post: 8016--><!--Time: 1108798400-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST8016"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, February 18, 2005 - 11:33 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tippy, thanks. Yes, that&#39;s interesting and a few years ago when I researched and wrote a paper on the klan I was amazed that states like Indiana had the most members, on a per capita basis. Oregon had seven times as many memebers as had West Virginia, believe it or not. <i>Hooded Americanism,</i>by David Chalmers. It&#39;s true that Texas and Oklahoma were the most violent klansmen; often that violence was in the form of what Texas, especially, called &#34;whipping bees.&#34; It is amazing what human beings will do when goaded on by others. This is the same dynamic which made fortunes for snake oil salesmen in the 19th Century and is at the core of violent gang activity. The need to belong even outweighs the drugs. Finally, regarding those states which had a low level on KKK in the 1920s &#40;I  don&#39;t mean any other version of KKK; the original KKK &#40;1866&#41; was my far the most deadly and brutal.&#41; They also often wore costumes and masks; many of these masks were square-cornered and resembled Zodiac at Lake Berryessa. Some of these masks also match the Texarkana Phantom. Finally, some of these masks match some of the ONS suspect description.  <BR> <BR>By the way, one last thing then I&#39;ll change topics. For anyone who is interested in the harrowing ONS case, there is news regarding that tape recording. 1&#41; The recording was left on an answering machine in 1978, not 1991. This is a verified fact. 2&#41; From tha potential ONS victim&#39;s answering machine tape alone, which doesn&#39;t run very long, a great number of people -- most of whom heard the tape on television -- have delared that ONS has a speech impediment. Hey, maybe he did. More likely, he disguised his voice. EAR/ONS, in my opinion, did more than any serial offender in the Twentieth Century than any other serial criminal. <BR> <BR>Beattie has problems. Hey, let&#39;s have Mr. Greysmith write a new, up-to-date story of the man <BR>known as BTK! Far better yet, let&#39;s have John Douglas write the book. No, he can&#39;t; he worked on the case at one time. Have Olson write the book. That&#39;s the ticket; he has the First Amendment on his side And BTK could do zero to stop such a book.  Boy would I like to imagine BTK squirming and suffeing. I&#39;m not keen on BTK being captured but would never wriote it off. I&#39;d like a blitzkreig, with BTK&#39;s ego in the cross hairs. Rattle him and he makes mistakes. Oh, but we musn&#39;t rattle him, he might go out and kill someone! Instead of rattling him, let&#39;s coddle him &#40;as if this hasn&#39;t already been dope for thirty-one years&#41;. By no means upset him. Poor BTK, everyone actually wants to capture him. Life just isn&#39;t fair, is it? <BR>Ivy_Leaguer. I&#39;ll try what you suggest. We&#39;ll see if ED is cool with it. It&#39;s no bombshell but I wanted to ask. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 8016-->
